  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_d445c74138764bf281db876aa21b7c09",
      "statement": "Uptime probing runs as a Cloudflare Worker on a one-minute cron trigger with a single D1 database binding, and D1 is deliberately the only storage because KV was judged unnecessary, simpler and cheaper to omit.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://worker/wrangler.toml#L1-L14",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fa4578d1a81bf5403062c8a92f63a9368c323303ef05dcda7af23cc4885f2b7d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_6dc3723310874c6eb16cb4a13026971b",
      "statement": "Each scheduled Worker run reads active monitors from D1, probes them all concurrently, batch-inserts the results into monitor_logs, and then reports state changes upward, returning early when no active monitors exist.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://worker/src/index.js#L1-L62",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:834cdb1b47e2b36c67ca28ef909cda16c091ccb4a16a8dfb535b993419631880:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_25004c314eed4c26a97f3ad7c3a9f186",
      "statement": "The Worker dispatches four probe types by monitor method — TCP ping, DNS, certificate check, and HTTP as the default.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://worker/src/index.js#L249-L260",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:65eaadffd873a598195dcf03fd45f3c5b2d1a15e5689b1c5aece2a4eb5fa2499:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3bf24eed17e34ffaaad0f3d407b61f6c",
      "statement": "HTTP probes enforce a per-monitor timeout defaulting to 10 seconds with AbortController, parse custom headers defensively so malformed header JSON logs and falls back to empty rather than failing the check, and include a request body only for methods that support one.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://worker/src/index.js#L262-L299",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:dde7dea23359ef0ffe276239c31d49fa95be838604271824347c3bcfad013735:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5f5a31cfe9884b50944c789057ecba59",
      "statement": "A probe passing its status check is still validated against required and forbidden response keywords, so a service returning a success status with an error page is correctly marked down.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://worker/src/index.js#L304-L325",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e90eb3c5740ec2588b118bf032b2791708c04f25fd442ca66e05710027892b1c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e2795b38a387405a8642fe14e5750eab",
      "statement": "The Worker sends a webhook only when a monitor's state changes between checks — trigger on up-to-down and resolve on down-to-up — explicitly suppressing repeat notifications while a monitor stays down, in order to prevent alert fatigue.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://worker/src/index.js#L142-L177",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5a00052db5882df0e55dd786fede65a74e969bc2bf508e87cd2080eef202c1fb:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_2809ef2725044c8b99f6ad7490abab1b",
      "statement": "A monitor with no recorded history is assumed to have been up, so its first failing check does produce a trigger event.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://worker/src/index.js#L155-L160",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:edc4510a6a78ee4a350fadc86d533eaa5d73dae0598cdead694fbf38d61f3be6:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4005c65ce04a42609ca2501ad26c0548",
      "statement": "Uptime events are emitted in the generic webhook format the Go API already parses, carrying alert name, severity, firing/resolved status, labels identifying the source as uptime-monitor, and the monitor id as the fingerprint — which lets existing alert deduplication and auto-resolution apply without special-casing.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://worker/src/index.js#L203-L247",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:073ba33300542a77f9582c5ccd6afb82e49bbbf0c610ff70237104630ec0505b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_45b9dd2563d949bb9f6b4e4898ac2eab",
      "statement": "Besides its cron handler the Worker serves a health endpoint and metrics, monitors and per-monitor stats APIs, caching successful responses in Cloudflare's default cache under ctx.waitUntil and reporting cache outcome through an X-Cache-Status header.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://worker/src/index.js#L65-L138",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:be5979beae60676b1e623dd7e87469462970bc44508e6322b35da24752a540c0:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d138cfbc524b42f38b6c1aa6101d0ac7",
      "statement": "The Worker chooses its reporting path by priority: an integration webhook URL when configured, otherwise a fallback webhook used only for monitors that are currently down, with the legacy /monitors/report endpoint retained only for backward compatibility.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://worker/src/index.js#L48-L63",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fd2fcd72f4edf2d05b723de1b05e50a1f4286501ee113d9f5d93518fa56a0a02:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7b453431af1e468e9026e89e105ee3fe",
      "statement": "The legacy report endpoint updates each monitor's last check time, status, latency, error and up flag, then creates an incident on an up-to-down transition and resolves the active one on a down-to-up transition, also creating an incident when a monitor with no prior state reports down.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://server/api/internal/monitor/report_handler.go#L39-L96",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:13e123c513021fca987f32a6bcc114a41a2e7c8209c6ae963645b615cc9ee229:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_dc34ce264e3947698079eb659cfdc48f",
      "statement": "Incidents created from uptime failures are titled 'Monitor Down' with high urgency, critical severity, source uptime-monitor, and the monitor's UUID stored in the incident's external id field, which is how the resolve path later finds them.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://server/api/internal/monitor/report_handler.go#L98-L116",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fa4f90d10753515eae35073ff64d45d8cf6ce2d21f5bd89847035071041caffc:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_739ef4e9208443b78526ebec7419bbce",
      "statement": "The uptime report endpoint does not yet validate the Authorization header against the deployment token, which is recorded as an outstanding TODO in the handler.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://server/api/internal/monitor/report_handler.go#L45-L50",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c18466b6d48486dbe3262fb6f9069c2c7d71283959c2182c8306d7a47f902d62:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1d7f120e6abf4ecba971455b4d66f266",
      "statement": "Resolving a monitor incident falls back to a direct SQL query because the incident listing API does not support filtering by external id, with the reasoning left inline as an unresolved working note.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://server/api/internal/monitor/report_handler.go#L117-L158",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:62d0645e911421eb4da95e9af4524836a81a4aa2761fde474d8b3f07efa4a9f1:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_c138f53946cb45f89b2e3d07b2532860",
      "statement": "The deployment endpoint provisions the uptime Worker into the user's own Cloudflare account, so the request supplies their account id and API token rather than using InRes-held credentials.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://server/api/internal/monitor/deployment_handler.go#L33-L52",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6a28548e4a1c73405016aa403e3a15980a49024b1528948163aa4ff650918705:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_f2df8e42c4e24eabad6b0712159a5cd2",
      "statement": "Deployment input validation is written around common user errors: whitespace and an accidental Bearer prefix are stripped, the account id must be exactly 32 characters with an error warning against passing a Zone ID, and a short token is rejected with a hint that a Global API Key is not an API Token.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://server/api/internal/monitor/deployment_handler.go#L46-L67",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:3f982563ebc330bee172805a62058350a5ffb99c7ea871d972c9f32e5dc364a5:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d5af00b6851f45c192f8f304b2212576",
      "statement": "A Cloudflare authentication failure during deployment is rewritten into an actionable message naming the three required token permissions: Workers Scripts edit, D1 edit and Account Settings read.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://server/api/internal/monitor/deployment_handler.go#L98-L110",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e8b22550b5c9e9b0d9237639fe3a729fe890291929d6334218fd51a0ebde1128:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_b3fd318c7dd0473ba45a00cc2156091c",
      "statement": "Deployment reuses an existing inres_DB D1 database when one is present rather than creating a new database on every deploy, then ensures its schema before uploading the Worker.",
